## Background tasks (Celery and Redis)

Redis serves as the Celery broker, stores task results, and is Django's cache.
Because the cache is shared, rate limits are counted across all server processes.

**Emails** ({% if two_factor %}OTP codes, {% endif %}password reset links) are sent by the worker
through `core.tasks.send_email`, so API requests don't wait for the mail server.
Failed sends are retried up to 5 times with increasing delays.

**Scheduled tasks** run through `celery beat`. The schedule is `CELERY_BEAT_SCHEDULE` in `conf/settings/base.py`:

| Task | Runs | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `flush_expired_tokens` | daily | Deletes expired refresh tokens from the blacklist tables |
{% if two_factor %}
| `delete_stale_otps` | hourly | Deletes used and expired one-time codes |
{% endif %}

**Add your own task** in any app's `tasks.py`:

```python
from celery import shared_task

@shared_task
def generate_report(report_id):
    ...

# call it without waiting:
generate_report.delay(report_id=42)
```

Pass IDs and plain values to tasks, not model instances.

The tests run tasks immediately and use an in-memory cache, so `pytest` doesn't need Redis.
{% endif %}

## Security defaults

- Access tokens last 15 minutes and refresh tokens 30 days. Refresh tokens are rotated and the old one is blacklisted.
- Inactive users can't log in, and their access tokens are rejected.
- Changing or resetting a password revokes every refresh token for that user.
- The auth endpoints are rate-limited (`THROTTLE_AUTH_RATE`, default `10/min`).
- Password reset responses don't reveal whether an email is registered.
- `conf.settings.production` requires `SECRET_KEY` and `ALLOWED_HOSTS` and turns on HTTPS-only cookies, HSTS and SSL redirect.

## Tests

```bash
pytest
```

## Deployment

Set `DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE=conf.settings.production`, plus `SECRET_KEY`,
`ALLOWED_HOSTS`, `DATABASE_URL`{% if celery %}, `REDIS_URL`{% endif %} and the email settings.
{% if docker %}
The `Dockerfile` builds a production image that serves the app with gunicorn on port 8000.
{% if celery %}
Run the same image twice more for the worker and the scheduler, with the commands
`celery -A conf worker -l info` and `celery -A conf beat -l info`. Run only one beat process.
{% endif %}
{% else %}
Serve the app with `gunicorn conf.wsgi:application` (listed in `requirements/production.txt`).
{% endif %}
Run `python manage.py migrate` whenever you deploy.
